The fix is in place, but I can't run the verifier — every attempt at `npx tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json` (and the local `tsc` binary, and the serena diagnostics tool) comes back as "requires approval" without a prompt I can satisfy. Please approve the command, or run it yourself.

## What I changed

`server/nervous-system/dynamics/claude-llm.ts` — the error was here:

```ts
thinkingContent = (block as unknown as Record<string, unknown>).thinking;  // unknown → string
```

Indexing `Record<string, unknown>` yields `unknown`, so the assignment to `string` failed. The double cast was also doing the `type === 'thinking'` check without giving the compiler anything to narrow with.

Replaced it with a real type guard (`server/nervous-system/dynamics/claude-llm.ts:153`):

```ts
interface ThinkingBlock {
  type: 'thinking';
  thinking: string;
}

function isThinkingBlock(block: unknown): block is ThinkingBlock {
  if (typeof block !== 'object' || block === null) {
    return false;
  }
  const candidate = block as Record<string, unknown>;
  return candidate.type === 'thinking' && typeof candidate.thinking === 'string';
}
```

and the loop becomes:

```ts
if (isThinkingBlock(block)) {
  thinkingContent = block.thinking;
} else if (block.type === 'text') {
  textContent = block.text;
}
```

No `any`, no suppression comments, and the runtime behavior is unchanged apart from the guard now also verifying `thinking` is actually a string before assigning it.

Why a local guard rather than the SDK's `ThinkingBlock`: `anthropic` here is `governedClaude` from `server/governance/governed-anthropic.ts:302`, whose `AnthropicResponse.content` is typed `Array<{ type: 'text'; text: string }>` — the bridge reconstructs responses itself and never declares thinking blocks. So the block has to be identified structurally at runtime; the guard makes that explicit instead of asserting it.

One thing worth flagging: because the governance bridge synthesizes its response from `extractLLMContent()` and emits a single text block, `thinkingContent` will always be `''` in practice — the guard can never match. That's a pre-existing behavior gap in the bridge, not something the type error was hiding, and I left it alone since fixing it means changing the governance layer's response shape.

